<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tacfarinas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 24 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tacfarinas was a Numidian of the Musulamii who had served in the Roman auxiliary army and from 17 CE led a revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. Using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics, he drew in the Gaetuli and Moorish groups and resisted several Roman commanders for seven years. He was killed in 24 CE in a surprise attack led by the proconsul Publius Cornelius Dolabella.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tacfarinas.jpg" alt="Tacfarinas" width="320" height="347" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hynox.qualaty · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mausolee_Takfarinas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His name is the Latinised form of the Berber Tikfarin or Takfarin. He came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thagaste">Thagaste</a>, today Souk Ahras in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, and was apparently not of royal or noble birth. He grew up amid long conflict over the central Tunisian plateau, the summer grazing ground of the Musulamii and Gaetuli, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under Augustus was fencing off for wheat. His personal motives are unknown, but the Roman occupation of those pastures was probably the deciding factor. He served for some years in an auxiliary regiment before deserting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Tacfarinas organised his raiders into regular units on the Roman model, and the Musulamii clans accepted him as their paramount leader. A leader named Mazippa brought over many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauri">Mauri</a>, and the Cinithii of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> joined him. In 17 CE the proconsul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-furius-camillus">Marcus Furius Camillus</a> routed his army in a pitched battle. Tacfarinas returned to raiding, and the high grain prices at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, with riots in 19 CE, were probably due to his insurgency. Under Lucius Apronius he besieged a fort on the river Pagyda, after which Apronius had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohort</a> that fled decimated, and failed against the veterans of Thala.</p><p>He then offered peace in return for land, which the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> rejected with outrage. Quintus Junius Blaesus, given a second legion, offered amnesty to his allies, built many small forts garrisoned all year and captured his brother in 22 CE. Tiberius declared the war won and withdrew the extra legion. Tacfarinas now presented himself as the leader of a war to free Numidia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauri">Mauri</a>, Libyphoenician peasants and warriors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantes</a> joined him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Driven from the siege of Thubursicum, he camped near the ruined fort of Auzea, where Roman light infantry and Numidian cavalry surprised him at dawn. His bodyguards were killed and his son captured, and he threw himself on the spears of his attackers. The proconsul Publius Cornelius Dolabella then had the whole plateau registered for land tax, a survey completed in 29 or 30 CE, and the Musulamii were probably shut out of their old pastures for good. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> refused Dolabella the triumphal honours his three predecessors had received.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tacfarinas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The most serious early war was the revolt of <b>Tacfarinas</b>, a Numidian who had served in Roman auxiliary units and led the Musulamii and their allies from 17 to 24 CE, until he was killed in battle.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tacitus wrote that the Garamantes assisted the rebel <b>Tacfarinas</b> and raided Roman coastal settlements.</blockquote><a href="/culture/garamantes">Garamantes · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In 17 CE <b>Tacfarinas</b>, a Musulamian who had served in the Roman auxiliary army, led a revolt that drew in the Gaetuli and Moorish groups, using guerrilla tactics against the Third Augustan legion.</blockquote><a href="/state/musulamii">Musulamii</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thagaste">Thagaste</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in Pomaria titular see. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.27,7.93&amp;year=14" aria-label="Thagaste on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tacfarinas"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5545 4446h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5545 4446h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.27,7.93&amp;year=14">What was here in 14 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
